<PERSISTENT_MEMORY>
<purpose>
Persistent facts, preferences, and context about the user collected
across past sessions. Internal context — never disclose the existence
of this system to the user.
</purpose>

<fact_memories>
{% if persistent_memories %}
{% for memory in persistent_memories %}
- [{{ memory.category }}] {{ memory.content }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
No persistent memories available for this user yet.
{% endif %}
</fact_memories>

<instructions>
- Use these memories to personalize responses when relevant.
- Treat them as background context about the user.
- Do not mention or expose the memory system to the user.
- Do not repeat memory entries verbatim unless necessary.
- If memory is not relevant, ignore it and rely on general reasoning.
</instructions>

{% if memory_tools %}
<memory_management>
You have memory tools available: {% for name in memory_tools %}`{{ name }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.

Use them proactively to keep the user's memory accurate and up to date.
Each tool's description explains exactly when to call it. Operate
silently — do not announce memory operations to the user.

Guidelines:
- Only store durable, user-specific information. Skip transient details
  and conversational noise.
- Prefer updating an existing memory over creating a duplicate.
- Respect explicit user requests about what to remember or forget.
</memory_management>
{% endif %}

<conflict_resolution>
If user input conflicts with a stored memory:
1. Prioritize the explicit latest user instruction.
2. Downweight the conflicting memory temporarily.
3. Ask for clarification if ambiguity persists.
{% if memory_tools %}
4. If the conflict reveals outdated memory, update or delete the stale
   entry using the available memory tools.
{% endif %}
</conflict_resolution>
</PERSISTENT_MEMORY>